course: dictionary
lesson: 1316
title: A reptile called the caiman
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A caiman is a reptile, a scaly animal from the alligator family. It lives in rivers, lakes and swamps in Central and South America. You can work out much of its meaning from the words around it. «The jaguar can crack turtle shells and caiman skulls» tells you a caiman has a strong, hard skull.
explain: Look at the brackets in «The Pantanal in Brazil is the world's biggest tropical wetland, where jaguars hunt caimans (cousins of crocodiles)». Cousins means related but not the same. So a caiman is close to a crocodile, but it is a different animal from the alligator family.
explain: Now try a swap. «Caimans' eyes glow red-orange in torchlight.» If glow means give out light, the sentence still makes sense, and that is why it is the right meaning. Always check your meaning fits the sentence.
fact: A caiman is a reptile of the alligator family.
fact: Caimans live in rivers, lakes and swamps in Central and South America.
fact: Caimans have long jaws and tough, scaly skin.
fact: Caimans are usually smaller than alligators and crocodiles, but the black caiman can grow very large.
fact: «Caimans' eyes glow red-orange in torchlight» because a shiny layer behind the eye reflects light.
exercise: «Caimans' eyes glow red-orange in torchlight.» What does glow mean? | Move quickly | Hide | Look wet | *Give out light
exercise: What is a caiman? | A bird that lives in trees | A fish with long fins | *A reptile of the alligator family | A large type of cat
exercise: «Caimans rarely attack big grown capybaras.» What does rarely mean? | Quickly | Loudly | Every day | *Not often
test: «The black caiman can grow very large.» What does this tell you about the black caiman? | It lives in the sea | It has no skin | It is always tiny | *It can get big
test: Jaguars hunt caimans in the Pantanal. Why do the brackets say caimans are cousins of crocodiles? | *They are related to crocodiles but not the same animal | They are crocodiles with a different name | They are birds | They are the same as jaguars
exam: Which clue in «Jaguars have one of the strongest bites of any cat for their size, strong enough to crack turtle shells» helps you understand the word strong? | Jaguars | Cat | *Crack turtle shells | Turtle
